A repeated failure mode is issuing successive demo requests on heterogeneous Continual-ARC pairs without integrating each response, then making an unsupported test guess. A generic instruction to "identify competing hypotheses" is too easy to skip. Before any next request, the solver should have explicitly compared exact diffs (including identity/no-op and unchanged structures), stated the strongest cross-demo relation and unresolved alternatives, and named a concrete observation that a new example could distinguish. If that comparison cannot be made, stop requesting and synthesize the current evidence first.

Postmortem references should not become proxy evidence about the current task. Load one only to answer a specific identified question, then return to the actual demo grids and verify the rule cell-by-cell. A previous session's outcome is a cautionary workflow note, not proof of this task's transformation.", "file_type": ".md", "_source_path": "/home/USER/continual-arc-baselines/.workbench/stage1/H-hermes-luna6-low-seq0/frozen/seen/play/users/u030/system/state/hermes/skills/reasoning/continual-arc-demo-integration-discipline/references/curator-review-lesson.md"}

{"success": true, "name": "continual-arc-error-recovery", "file": "references/local-demo-global-rule.md", "content": "# Separating a demo's local effect from its global rule

A minimal example may reveal what happens to particular cells without proving how those cells are selected in larger grids. In this session, the 2×10 pair established that a trailing run of 9s becomes 8s and that part of a run remains unchanged, but it did not validate a whole-grid operation for a separate 22×25 test. The first submission simply erased the test's 1-cells; rejection falsified that extrapolation. The next submission erased all test marks except an isolated vertical pair, without a demonstration supporting that selection, and was also unsupported.

When demo and test scales differ sharply, separate and validate: (1) local value mapping, (2) trigger/selection, (3) propagation or geometry, and (4) scope. Keep unchanged demo cells as constraints. If multiple global generators still fit, request a demo designed to distinguish them rather than submitting a conjecture. Never infer an exception mask solely from a visually salient test pattern.", "file_type": ".md", "_source_path": "/home/USER/continual-arc-baselines/.workbench/stage1/H-hermes-luna6-low-seq0/frozen/seen/play/users/u030/system/state/hermes/skills/reasoning/continual-arc-error-recovery/references/local-demo-global-rule.md"}

{"success": true, "name": "continual-arc-error-recovery", "file": "references/trajectory-after-error.md", "content": "# Revising sparse trajectory hypotheses after a wrong output

A wrong submission that extends only one apparent ray is not repaired by shifting its start or changing a single cell. Re-derive the generator from each demo by recording every seed marker, color, direction vector, and endpoint. In the example reviewed here, one demo was identity (a sparse object did not trigger extension), while the larger demo showed simultaneous rays: a 7-ray extended down-right from its marker, and 6-ray(s) extended along the other diagonal direction from multiple marker locations. The test had several 9 markers plus paired 7 and 6 markers; assuming the salient 9-marker cluster was irrelevant and extending just one 6 ray / one short 7 ray was unsupported and was rejected. General lesson: compare the full input geometry to positive and negative demos; infer whether multiple marker colors or every compatible seed launches a ray, and project each ray through the full grid to the evidenced boundary. Preserve original objects and background. Do not submit a one-color or one-ray interpretation unless it accounts for all trajectory behavior in the demonstrations.

This example is not a universal rule for launching rays from all markers: use it to formulate competing hypotheses, then select the one that explains the complete demos and test configuration.", "file_type": ".md", "_source_path": "/home/USER/continual-arc-baselines/.workbench/stage1/H-hermes-luna6-low-seq0/frozen/seen/play/users/u030/system/state/hermes/skills/reasoning/continual-arc-error-recovery/references/trajectory-after-error.md"}

{"success": true, "name": "continual-arc-error-recovery", "file": "references/ray-inference-failure.md", "content": "# Ray extension inference after failed submissions

When a ray-extension prediction is rejected, reject the entire prior generator instead of making a cosmetic grid edit. Re-derive each ray independently from the demos: identify its seed/anchor coordinates, direction vector, color, whether the seed is part of the ray, and the stopping boundary. Track coexisting rays separately; one demo's ray color or direction must not be generalized to all marker groups without evidence. For a test with multiple potential ray seeds, enumerate candidate rays and validate each against every demo, including unchanged markers and endpoints. If the test has a configuration not represented in demonstrations, spend a demo only when it distinguishes explicitly named alternatives. Validate that generated cells do not overwrite given objects unless outputs prove overwrite behavior.
